Additions to the bru.doc file that none of my editors will insert, they  take the backspaces back out of Michael's "printer ready" files.BRU now has (in the backup mode) a -s option.BRU -s /dd/musik/pan (along with the other usual command line stuff)  will  cause  bru  to scan the source drive until it finds  the  named  file.  On  finding the named file, it turns on a flag that has prevented  it  from writing any previous data to the target disk up to this point.  By  this control, we can restart a crashed or otherwise failed backup  at the point of the last stoppage or failure..cp7  To  take advantage of this rapidly, use the -vvvv options and  either  log  it to a non-paused screen, or to the printer. You will then have  a record of how far you got. To restart it give it the -s lastname on  list  option, on the same disk if its not tail damaged, or on a fresh  one if it is reported as damaged by the bru -t option.  It will start  at  that  same  file which will on recovery be  overwritten  and  any  damage done by the stoppage or crash thereby repaired..cp10  Note  that if the backup was interrupted at the "gimme another  disk"  prompt,   bru  does splice across a disk swap seemlessly!   The  last  file  on  that disk stands a 99.44% chance of being  incomplete!   In  order  for the -s option to work correctly, its necessary to  re-read  the  whole  source  device  so  that all  the  recursion  levels  are  maintained.   Once  the -s "filename" has been found and  the  output  path enabled, it starts at the beginning of the named file, not where  the previous backup was interrupted.  So you must start with the last  file  shown on the interrupted backup in order to generate an archive  that will correctly overwrite that partial file with the full file..cp9And I made one other change that shows up if the -q option isn't used.   Michael's code asked for the keypress to indicate that a new disk had  been  inserted  from  the  path 'stderr'. On my  system,  even  if  I  attempted  redirection, it failed by sailing right on by what  should  have  been  a 'stopped & waiting for a keypress' situation  which  in  turn  ran  the disk forever checking it for more room in the  FAT  it  didn't   have any of!  The drive got rather warm in an hour of  that!  So  this version now asks for the keypress from 'stdin', path 0.  And  on my system '<<' is an error anyway (using shell+)..cp11Change #3, the most recent.  One of the people who dloaded the original  1.1 version didn't seem to understand the command line syntax to make  it use an archive path other than "/d1/brufile", and couldn't seem to  get  it to do a restart either.  I've double-checked the  performance  of   the  -s  option several times here without  any  problems.  This  person  was  using the WDDisk series of drivers and his floppy  names  were  different,  as in "/ld3" instead of "/d3".  As BRU is  sort  of  hard coded for "/d1/brufile" unless you use the -f option, he may not  have  understood that the -f option must always be used regardless of  what   other  options  are  in  use  if  the  backup  target   wasn't  /d1/brufile..cp7  Because   thats  a  pain, and I like painless software myself, I  did  incorporate  the idea of a fixed location file that tells BRU (if the  file  is  there) to use the device named in the file for  the  target  ^b/device/filename^b  that will contain the removable media.  To make use  of  that, make a file in the /dd/sys directory that contains one line  only, ^bmaximum of 31 characters^b, which is the name of the /device/file  etc you want BRU to use ^bon your system^b..cp7I'm  using it here, usually run in the wee hours by cronV9, my "uses  a  data  module  for  crontab"  version of  a  disassembled  Pete  Lyall  original,  heavily patched to get rid on one very messy bug, it  used  the  same buffer memory as the line was read into to build its output  command  line, usually trashing the command it was supposed to run. I  fixed  that,  made  it check both /r0 and /dd/sys for  its  file.  It  worked ok but took 11+k of smap memory!.cp9That   didn't leave me with much so I got to thinking what might happen  if  I made a data module, merged it to cron etc, then attempt a  link  with it? That works great, and I've now got 11k of smap left when its  running!  That was a worthwhile plus. The thing is up on Delphi along  with  the  rest  of the tools to make making the datamod a  piece  of  cake.  All the user needs is an editor and the old level one asm with  level  two  os9defs, and ezgen to really fly. Even without ezgen  its  only a few minutes work. Just keep a copy of cron by itself and merge  it and the datamod to 'cronutils', load and exec with an ampersand..cp2Search on 'Cron9'. And for the print formatter that did this all in one  run, search on 'Printform2_3', thats at least 75% my code now..cp7Reply to the same old address,                    Gene Heskett                    291 Garton Avenue                    Weston, WV 26452Landline 304-269-4295 nights, Delphi 'WOAY'